     "solution": "1. Primary capabilities\n- What is a case?\n- What kinds of grader?\n- What does the report hold?\n\n2. Error handling\n- A grader can't tell, such as a judge that says 'unsure'?\n- The system under test crashes on a case?\n- The grader itself crashes?\n\n3. Scope boundaries\n- How do we know it got worse?\n- A real model, a database, a UI?\n- Reruns, human labels, parallel runs?",
     "why": "Every question changes a class or a rule. Three grader kinds give three grader classes, 'can't tell' gives Score an Unknown, and the two crashes give _run_case its split between 0.0 and Unknown. The baseline question gives compare.",
     "say": "**I sort my questions into three buckets: what it must do, what goes wrong, where scope stops.** I ask what a case holds and which graders exist, because that decides the classes. Then the three failures: a grader that can't tell, a system that crashes, a grader that crashes. Those are three different outcomes, and getting them right is most of the design."
